## Adding a resource

Generate a complete REST resource (model, validation, storage, service, handlers, and
tests) with [GoForge](https://github.com/Frank5ive/goforge):

```bash
goforge add resource product --fields "name:string,price:float,in_stock:bool"
```

This creates a `products/` package with a paginated `GET /products`, `POST /products`, and
`GET/PUT/PATCH/DELETE /products/{id}`, plus a `products/requests.http` file of example
requests. The routes are registered in `server/routes.go` for you, so they work as soon as
you restart the server. Data is stored in memory until you implement `products.Repository`
for a database.

## Adding an endpoint

1. Add a handler in `handlers/`:

   ```go
   // Greet responds with a greeting for the {name} path value.
   func Greet() http.HandlerFunc {
       return func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
           WriteJSON(w, http.StatusOK, map[string]string{
               "message": "Hello, " + r.PathValue("name") + "!",
           })
       }
   }
   ```

2. Register it in `server/routes.go`:

   ```go
   mux.HandleFunc("GET /greet/{name}", handlers.Greet())
   ```

3. Add a test next to it in `handlers/`, then run `make test`.

Route patterns use Go 1.22+ routing: the method in the pattern is enforced (other
methods get `405 Method Not Allowed`) and `{name}` segments are read with
`r.PathValue`.
